nseen, but B9 has been played by next player (B9 in their history) and across player (B9 in history), so likely safe.  \n- T5, T7, T8: These are mid-suit tiles; T5 has 3 left, T7 has 3 left, T8 has 2 left.  \n- T2: has 3 left; T2 has been played by previous player (T2 in history).  \n- W7: has 3 left; across player has W7 in meld, so maybe safe.  \n\nBest discard: I want to keep tiles that can form sequences. T7 and T8 can combine if I draw T6 or T9. T5 can combine with T4 or T6. T2 is more isolated.  \nW7 is isolated in my hand (only one, no adjacent W6 or W8, and W6 is mostly used by others).  \n\nI think discard W7 is relatively safe and doesn't break potential sequences in my hand (since my sequences are in T suits mostly). Also, keeping B6 pair is good.  \n\nReason: W7 is less useful for forming chows in my current hand, and based on melds, W7 may be safer because across player already has a W6-W7-W8 chow meld, reducing their need for W7. Also, unshown W7 count is 3, so not too rare.  \n\nFinal choice: Play W7.  \n\nAnswer: Play W7"}
